      "version": "V4",
      "markdown": "# Find Prospects\n\nBuild deduplicated, prioritized prospect lists by discovering net-new target accounts, identifying relevant people at known accounts, or doing both. Use for territory building, account discovery, persona-aligned contact discovery, buying-committee coverage, lookalike prospecting, or list refreshes; do not use for deep account research, outreach drafting or sending, meeting preparation, or pipeline management.\n\n## Purpose\n\nProduce a reviewable list of accounts and/or people who match the organization's target criteria, show relevant timing, and are not hidden duplicates or conflicts in CRM.\n\nUse three modes:\n\n- Account discovery: find and rank net-new target accounts.\n- Contact discovery: find and rank relevant people at specified or CRM accounts.\n- Account + contact discovery: find qualified accounts, then identify the right people within them.\n\nTreat the GTM Profile as the definition of organization-level fit, signals, anti-ICP classes, and personas. Treat CRM and external sources as evidence about each candidate. Named-account or contact restrictions, ownership, customer status, do-not-contact state, and collision rules belong in CRM configuration or policy. Never bake customer-specific criteria, titles, signals, vendor preferences, or suppression rules into this skill.\n\n## Trigger Logic\n\nRun this skill when the user asks to:\n\n- Find companies, accounts, leads, buyers, decision-makers, champions, or other prospects.\n- Build or refresh a territory, target-account list, contact list, or buying committee.\n- Find people with a particular responsibility at one or more known accounts.\n- Find accounts similar to a supplied customer or seed account.\n- Surface net-new prospects showing a relevant buying signal.\n\nInfer mode from the request:\n\n- \"Find companies/accounts\" -> Account discovery.\n- \"Find people/contacts at these accounts\" -> Contact discovery.\n- \"Build a prospect list\" with no object specified -> ask whether the user wants accounts, people at known accounts, or both.\n\nAsk one focused question only when the mode is ambiguous or when a missing territory, target cohort, or required delivery field would materially change the list. Mode is blocking because it changes cost, scope, and output.\n\n## Boundaries and Handoffs\n\nFor every handoff, pass the target record identifiers, bounded goal, user-selected or approved state, evidence references with dates, relevant constraints, and requested return format. Return control to the originating workflow when the handed-off result is needed to finish the request.\n\nFor net-new outbound, use this order: [Find Prospects](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-find-prospects) identifies and verifies candidates; [Account Research](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-account-research-summary) deepens evidence only for user-selected accounts when needed; [Write Sales Emails](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-write-sales-emails) drafts only after the recipient is selected.\n\n- Use [Account Research](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-account-research-summary) for a deep, evidence-backed brief on shortlisted accounts.\n- Use [Write Sales Emails](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-write-sales-emails) after the user selects prospects and requests outreach.\n- Use [Pre-Meeting Prep](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-prepare-for-meetings) for scheduled customer interactions.\n- Use [Pipeline Management](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-pipeline-management) for live opportunity health and progression.\n- Use [Data Sourcing](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-data-sourcing) to select connected providers, enrichment recipes, waterfalls, validation, and fallbacks. Do not embed those choices here.\n- Do not infer or manufacture email addresses, phone numbers, employment, intent, or account fit.\n- Do not create CRM records, enroll sequences, or send messages unless the user separately requests the action and policy permits it.\n\n## Inputs\n\nResolve these inputs before sourcing.\n\n**Required or inferable**\n\n- Mode: account, contact, or account + contact discovery.\n- Scope: geography, segment, product, territory, named accounts, CRM view, or other target universe. Use GTM Profile defaults when the request omits scope.\n- Desired volume: use customer-configured defaults when available.\n\n**Optional**\n\n- Seed accounts, lookalike examples, explicit inclusions, or exclusions.\n- Required roles, responsibilities, seniority, buying-committee coverage, or persona.\n- Timing-signal requirement and lookback window.\n- Required contact channels or fields.\n- CRM owner, campaign, list, segment, or assignment context.\n- User-supplied candidates to enrich, validate, or deduplicate.\n\nIf neither the user nor Knowledge defines volume, use these defaults:\n\n- Account discovery: 25 accounts.\n- Contact discovery: up to 3 relevant people per account and 50 people total.\n- Account + contact discovery: 15 accounts and up to 2 relevant people per account.\n\nTreat these as output limits, not quotas. Return fewer candidates rather than weaken a hard criterion or fabricate data.\n\n## Knowledge Hydration\n\nLoad only the GTM Profile sections relevant to the selected mode. The platform resolves every link below and injects the section content into the runtime context.\n\n**Always load**\n\n- [GTM Profile -> Profile Scope](#gtm-profile-profile-scope)\n- [GTM Profile -> Target Accounts](#gtm-profile-target-accounts)\n\n**Load for account or account + contact discovery**\n\n- [GTM Profile -> Buying Signals](#gtm-profile-buying-signals)\n\n**Load for contact or account + contact discovery**\n\n- [GTM Profile -> Buyer Personas](#gtm-profile-buyer-personas)\n- [GTM Profile -> Buying Process](#gtm-profile-buying-process)\n\n**Load when relevance or prioritization requires it**\n\n- [GTM Profile -> Company and Offer](#gtm-profile-company-and-offer)\n- [GTM Profile -> Customer Problems and Use Cases](#gtm-profile-customer-problems-and-use-cases)\n- [GTM Profile -> Positioning](#gtm-profile-positioning)\n- [GTM Profile -> Objections](#gtm-profile-objections)\n\nExtract hard criteria, soft indicators, organization-level anti-ICP classes, signal definitions and windows, persona responsibilities, and buying roles. Knowledge guides candidate selection; it does not prove a candidate meets a criterion. Read named-account and contact restrictions, ownership, customer state, and do-not-contact rules from CRM configuration and policy.\n\nIf a required section is missing:\n\n1. Use explicit constraints in the user's request.\n2. Do not invent ICP or persona criteria.\n3. Return a clearly labeled partial result if it can still be useful.\n4. State which Knowledge section the admin should complete.\n\nIf a section is marked stale, superseded, draft, or low-confidence, preserve that status. Do not use uncertain guidance as a hard filter or decisive ranking factor; place affected candidates in Review unless the remaining current evidence supports a decision.\n\n## Source and Tool Contract\n\nRead Data Sourcing; the platform injects its content into the same runtime context. This skill defines who and what to find. Data Sourcing defines which providers and actions to call, waterfall order, validation, freshness, cost limits, and stopping conditions.\n\nUse the Data Gateway only to invoke exposed provider actions. The gateway contains no business logic. Never hard-code provider names or assume that a specific account, contact, email, intent, or enrichment action is connected.\n\n**Source roles**\n\n1. CRM: use first for deduplication, existing relationships, ownership, customer status, active opportunities, prior prospecting, contact restrictions, and suppression.\n2. Connected data providers: use according to Data Sourcing for account discovery, contact discovery, enrichment, verification, and provider-reported signals.\n3. Company first-party sources: use to verify domains, company identity, locations, products, hiring, and named leadership or employees when available.\n4. Authoritative external sources: use for dated events that support a buying signal.\n5. Other web sources: use for discovery or corroboration, not as the sole basis for a material decision when stronger evidence exists.\n\n**Evidence standard**\n\nRetain the following for every candidate:\n\n- Canonical identity: company name and domain; for people, full name, current company, and current title or responsibility.\n- Evidence for every hard-fit criterion and any claimed buying signal.\n- CRM match or an explicit \"No match found,\" plus the matching keys used.\n- Source URL or provider/internal record reference, source type, observed or published date, retrieval date, and confidence.\n- For contact methods, provider verification status and verification date.\n\nNever output a guessed or pattern-generated email address or phone number as verified. Label provider-modeled intent and inferred responsibilities explicitly.\n\n**Freshness defaults**\n\nFollow GTM Profile or Data Sourcing rules when present. Otherwise:\n\n- Employment and title: prefer verification within 30 days.\n- Email or phone validity: prefer verification within 90 days.\n- Time-sensitive buying signals: prefer evidence within 90 days.\n- Firmographics: prefer evidence observed within 180 days.\n\nPreserve older data as stale only when it remains useful. When sources conflict, preserve the conflict, prefer first-party and newer evidence for public facts, and lower confidence rather than selecting the convenient answer.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Set the mode and limit. Infer or confirm account, contact, or account + contact discovery; scope; desired volume; required fields; and as-of time.\n2. Hydrate Knowledge. Extract target-account criteria, exclusions, signals, personas, and buying roles. Read territory, ownership, and suppression behavior from live CRM configuration and the CRM Writeback Policy rather than inventing it here.\n3. Build a selection spec separating hard filters, soft indicators, explicit exclusions and inverse signals, persona responsibilities and buying roles, and required evidence and freshness.\n4. Build the CRM suppression set. Retrieve existing accounts, leads, contacts, customers, active opportunities, ownership, recent touches, campaign/list membership, bounce status, do-not-contact flags, and configured exclusions. Record counts by suppression reason.\n5. Generate candidate accounts. Use Data Sourcing rules to search the allowed universe. For lookalikes, derive the relevant attributes from Knowledge and independently verify that each candidate meets them; similarity alone is not qualification.\n6. Resolve and verify accounts. Confirm the canonical domain, parent/subsidiary relationship, hard-fit criteria, exclusions, and signal evidence. Do not spend on contact enrichment for an account that fails a hard filter.\n7. Generate candidate people. In contact modes, search only retained or supplied accounts. Match responsibilities and buying roles from Buyer Personas, not exact title strings alone. Include multiple roles only when they provide useful buying-committee coverage.\n8. Verify employment and contactability. Confirm current employer and role. Enrich only the contact fields required by the request. Preserve verification dates and return \"Not verified\" instead of guessing.\n9. Deduplicate deterministically. Match accounts by CRM ID when present, then canonical domain and parent/subsidiary. Match people by CRM ID, normalized email, or stable professional profile URL; use full name plus company only as a review candidate, never an automatic merge key.\n10. Apply suppression visibly. Exclude configured conflicts from the main list, but report the candidate and reason in an exclusions summary. If no rule governs an existing CRM relationship, flag it for review rather than silently excluding it.\n11. Rank the retained candidates. Apply customer-defined weighting when present. Otherwise use the default decision logic below.\n12. Check coverage. Confirm the result meets the requested account count, persona mix, buying-role mix, geography, and required field coverage without weakening hard criteria.\n13. Stop deliberately. Stop at the requested limit, configured cost/time ceiling, or when additional providers repeat candidates. Report shortfalls and the binding constraint.\n14. Produce the list. Put qualified candidates first, keep evidence adjacent to claims, and make the next handoff explicit.\n\nParallelize independent candidate searches and enrichments where safe. Resolve entities before deduplication or paid enrichment.\n\n## Decision Logic\n\nApply explicit GTM Profile, CRM configuration, and policy rules before these defaults.\n\n**Candidate status**\n\n- Qualified: all hard filters are supported, no exclusion is present, CRM conflict is cleared, and required identity fields are verified.\n- Review: likely fit, but one non-critical criterion, employment fact, CRM match, or buying-role interpretation remains uncertain.\n- Excluded: a hard exclusion, duplicate, current-customer rule, open-opportunity conflict, ownership rule, contact restriction, or explicit user constraint applies.\n- Insufficient data: a required identity, fit, or contactability field cannot be verified.\n\n**Default ranking**\n\nRank using these dimensions in order unless Knowledge supplies weights:\n\n1. Hard-fit confidence.\n2. Current, specific Knowledge-defined buying signal.\n3. Persona and buying-role alignment.\n4. Clean CRM status and territory ownership.\n5. Evidence and contact-data freshness.\n\nDo not rank a person higher only because of seniority. Prefer the person whose responsibilities and role in the buying process best match the GTM Profile. Use uncovered buying roles as a tie-breaker when building a committee.\n\nDo not weaken a hard filter to hit volume. If the qualified set is too small, report which constraint binds and ask whether the user wants to change it.\n\n## Output Requirements\n\n- State the mode, requested volume, actual volume, scope, as-of time, and Knowledge sections used.\n- Show qualified and review candidates separately from exclusions.\n- Explain why every included account fits and, when applicable, why it is timely.\n- Explain why every included person maps to a hydrated persona or buying role.\n- Show the CRM match, owner, and conflict status for every candidate.\n- Include only verified contact fields requested by the user; label unavailable or stale fields.\n- Cite hard-fit facts, signals, employment, and contact verification with sources and dates.\n- Include a suppression and deduplication summary; never silently drop a sourced candidate.\n- End with a clear handoff, such as Account Research on the top accounts or Write Sales Emails for user-selected contacts.\n\n## Output Format\n\nStart every result with:\n\n```\n# Prospect List: {scope or objective}\n**Mode:** {Account / Contact / Account + Contact}\n**As of:** {date and time}\n**Requested / returned:** {count} / {count}\n**Scope:** {territory, segment, named accounts, or other universe}\n**Coverage:** {required fields and completion rate}\n**Knowledge used:** {linked GTM Profile sections}\n\n### Qualified Accounts\n| Rank | Account | Domain | Fit | Why now | CRM status / owner | Confidence | Evidence |\n|---:|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | {account} | {domain} | {matched criteria} | {signal and date, or \"No current signal required\"} | {status / owner} | {High / Medium / Low} | {[S#]} |\n\n### Qualified People\n| Rank | Person | Current role | Account | Persona / buying role | Why this person | Verified contactability | CRM status | Evidence |\n|---:|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | {name} | {title} | {account} | {mapping} | {responsibility-based rationale} | {requested fields, status, and date} | {status / owner} | {[S#]} |\n\nOmit the accounts or people table when the selected mode does not produce it. In account + contact mode, keep people grouped or sortable by account.\n\n### Review Required\n| Candidate | Open question | Why it matters | Best next verification |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| {candidate} | {missing or conflicting fact} | {decision impact} | {specific action} |\n\n### Exclusions and Dedupe Summary\n- **Sourced candidates:** {count}\n- **Qualified:** {count}\n- **Review:** {count}\n- **Excluded:** {count by duplicate, customer, active opportunity, territory, restriction, hard-fit, stale/unverified, and other}\n\n| Candidate | Disposition | Reason | CRM or evidence reference |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| {candidate} | {Excluded / Merged / Suppressed} | {specific rule} | {[S#] or CRM link} |\n\n### Suggested Handoff\n{One recommended next action and the exact subset it applies to.}\n\n### Sources\n- **[S1]** {source label} - {clickable URL or internal/provider record reference}; {published/observed/verified date}; retrieved {date}; {source type}\n```\n\n## Actions and Writeback\n\n- Keep CRM and sourcing tools read-only by default.\n- Follow CRM Writeback Policy before creating or updating accounts, leads, contacts, list membership, campaigns, ownership, or prospecting status.\n- When the user requests writeback, preview the destination object, dedupe key, exact field values, source, owner, and collision behavior before mutation unless policy explicitly pre-authorizes it.\n- Create no CRM record for a Review or Insufficient data candidate unless the user explicitly accepts the gap.\n- Never overwrite verified contact data with stale data, rep-authored context with enrichment, or another seller's ownership.\n- After any policy-permitted write, return one result per target record with status, record ID, old and new values or action, source, error if any, and whether a failed item is safe to retry. Never retry a succeeded item.\n- Never enroll a sequence, draft an email, or send a message from this skill.\n\n## Quality Gate\n\nDo not deliver until all applicable checks pass:\n\n- [ ] Mode, scope, volume, required fields, and as-of time are explicit.\n- [ ] The relevant GTM Profile sections were loaded, or missing sections are disclosed.\n- [ ] Hard filters, soft indicators, exclusions, signals, and persona responsibilities are separated.\n- [ ] Account identities, canonical domains, and parent/subsidiary relationships are resolved.\n- [ ] CRM dedupe, ownership, customer, opportunity, recent-touch, and contact-restriction checks ran.\n- [ ] Every included candidate maps to hydrated account or persona criteria.\n- [ ] Material claims, signals, employment, and contact verification have sources and dates.\n- [ ] Inferred, modeled, stale, missing, and conflicting data is labeled.\n- [ ] No email address, phone number, employment, or intent was guessed.\n- [ ] Suppressed and merged candidates are counted and explained.\n- [ ] Hard criteria were not relaxed to hit volume.\n- [ ] The output is prioritized, scan-friendly, and ready for the stated handoff.\n- [ ] No CRM record, sequence, draft, or message was created without a separate authorized action.\n\n## Feedback Routing\n\nClassify durable feedback before proposing a change:\n\n- Which account classes, personas, buying roles, signals, or anti-ICP categories should our business care about? Propose an edit to the relevant GTM Profile section in Knowledge.\n- How should prospect discovery, qualification, ranking, deduplication, or coverage work? Propose an edit to this Find Prospects skill.\n- How should providers, waterfalls, enrichment fields, verification, freshness, or fallback work? Propose an edit to Data Sourcing.\n- Which provider actions are available or connected? Route to Data Gateway connector and tool configuration; do not add business logic there.\n- What is true about a specific account or person, or should that named record be restricted? Propose an evidence-backed CRM update under CRM Writeback Policy.\n- How may the agent create or update CRM records? Propose an edit to CRM Writeback Policy.\n- Is this instruction only for the current request? Apply it in-session and do not persist it.\n\nFor a durable change, present the destination, exact proposed change, and expected impact through the product's approval flow. Never hide customer-specific targeting or persona definitions inside this skill.\n",
