      "updatedAt": "yesterday at 4:00 PM",
      "version": "V8",
      "markdown": "# Pipeline Snapshot\n\nGet a concise daily view of your Salesforce pipeline—what changed, what needs attention, and which actions will have the greatest impact.\n\n## What this skill does\n\nPipeline Snapshot analyzes your Salesforce opportunities and creates a prioritized briefing for the day. It highlights meaningful pipeline movement, emerging risks, stalled deals, and recommended next actions so you can focus on the opportunities that need you most.\n\n## How this skill works\n\nCoordinates three supporting Skills to build the snapshot:\n\n[Review Deal Health](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-review-deal-health) [Identify Pipeline Changes](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-identify-pipeline-changes) [Recommend Next Actions](/skill_detail.html?id=sk-recommend-next-actions)\n\n## Instructions\n\n1. Analyze the current user's open opportunities for the current quarter by default. If the user specifies a different time period, segment, territory, or pipeline view, use that scope instead.\n2. Retrieve the seller's open opportunities from Salesforce.\n3. Default to records owned by the current user and expected to close this quarter.\n4. Compare the current pipeline with the latest available snapshot.\n5. Identify material changes in stage, amount, probability, close date, and status.\n6. Evaluate momentum, engagement, risk, and next-step readiness.\n7. Prioritize high-impact opportunities that are immediately actionable.\n8. Recommend a specific next action for each priority opportunity.\n9. Explain risks and recommendations using available Salesforce evidence.\n10. Clearly separate confirmed facts from inferred risks.\n11. Never update records or contact customers without explicit approval.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Never invent customer activity, deal changes, or pipeline values.\n- Label recommendations and inferred risks clearly.\n- Show data source and freshness when relevant.\n- Exclude closed opportunities unless they changed during the comparison period.\n- Respect Salesforce permissions and sharing rules.\n- Ask for clarification when pipeline scope is ambiguous.\n\n## Example output\n\nA typical morning briefing surfaces the two or three opportunities where a specific next action is most likely to move the deal forward, each paired with the pipeline signal that raised it and a suggested draft the seller can accept or edit before sending.\n",
      "linked": true
    },
