      "version": "V3",
      "markdown": "# Prepare for Meetings\n\nResearches the account, attendees, recent activity, and open opportunities to create a concise meeting brief before every customer conversation.\n\n## What this skill does\n\nAhead of each scheduled meeting with a customer or prospect, Prepare for Meetings assembles a one-page brief: attendees and their roles, the account's current state in Salesforce, open opportunities, recent activity across email and calendar, and the two or three most important questions to ask.\n\n## Instructions\n\n1. Pull upcoming meetings from Google Calendar for the current day and the following business day.\n2. For each meeting, identify the linked Salesforce account and any linked opportunities.\n3. Enrich the attendee list with role, title, and last-touch information from Salesforce.\n4. Summarize the account's current state in three to five bullets—stage, momentum, key stakeholders, open risks.\n5. List the last five relevant activities (calls, emails, meetings, notes) in reverse chronological order.\n6. Propose two or three prepared questions grounded in the current opportunity stage.\n7. Deliver the brief no later than 15 minutes before the meeting starts.\n\n## Guardrails\n\n- Include only information the seller is authorized to view in Salesforce.\n- Do not surface private notes flagged as sensitive.\n- Skip meetings marked as personal in the seller's calendar.\n- Never send the brief to attendees; it is for the seller only.\n\n## Example output\n\nThe day-before brief lists the two most consequential meetings, a five-bullet snapshot of each account, the attendees with roles, and three prepared questions tuned to the current stage.\n",
